  "worked_solution": "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=7,$ $b=3,$ and $c=5.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(7,3)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}7 & = & 3\\cdot 2 & + & 1 \\\\  & \u2199 &  & \u2199 &  \\\\ 3 & = & 1\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =7-3\\cdot 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n7+3\\cdot (-2)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+3\\cdot (-2) & =1 \\\\ 7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 5 & =5 \\\\ 7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot (-10) & =5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(5,-10)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(5+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-10-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(5+3t,\\,-10-7t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $y=-10-7t$ is attained when $t=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{\u223c}{y}=-10-7\\cdot (-2)=4.\n$$",
